WEBVTT

00:00.440 --> 00:12.920
Alright y'all, in this video I want to talk about something that I think is often overlooked by traders, especially those who are newer to trading, newer to using Market Cipher, and that is finding good entries.

00:13.520 --> 00:27.520
Finding a good entry for your trades is one of the best risk management practices that you can have because what it does is it allows you to keep the losses very, very small and the wins very big.

00:27.520 --> 00:40.380
If you're entering trade after trade and you're breaking even or having a very small loss, but then you're having very big wins also, that's going to help your overall profit and loss statement, right?

00:40.400 --> 00:44.160
You're going to be much more profitable if you can keep those losses to a minimum.

00:44.520 --> 00:54.440
And the way we're going to do that is by finding good entries that allow us to risk very, very little while having the potential to win a lot.

00:54.440 --> 01:01.040
And the way we're going to do that is we need to have some market structure to trade off of.

01:01.820 --> 01:08.780
Something that a lot of people overlook is just because Market Cipher is giving you a buy signal doesn't mean it's the best place for you to buy.

01:09.020 --> 01:14.940
Because the reality is, although Market Cipher is a great tool and we can use it to enter some really good trades,

01:14.940 --> 01:27.020
cryptocurrency is extremely, extremely volatile and it's very possible that you can get wicked out of a trade and it's very discouraging for a lot of people who are longing or shorting

01:27.020 --> 01:36.540
and they end up right on the money in terms of their projection of the price action, but they get wicked out because their stop loss was in the wrong place

01:36.540 --> 01:39.160
or maybe they just had a really bad entry.

01:39.660 --> 01:44.020
And what our goal is, is to look for trade setups where we can enter a trade.

01:44.340 --> 01:45.420
Let's say we're entering long.

01:45.780 --> 01:55.420
Our goal should be to enter a trade in a place where we can have a lot of potential reward with a very, very small amount of risk.

01:55.420 --> 02:03.760
A lot of people know if you're in the course, I talk about not risking more than 1-5% of your entire trading account per trade

02:03.760 --> 02:07.740
and finding a good entry will allow you to do that.

02:07.860 --> 02:08.940
It will allow you to do that.

02:09.060 --> 02:14.360
And you might find yourself sitting out of certain trades, but you'll find in the long run that it will pay off

02:14.360 --> 02:20.640
because you will actually find yourself winning more trades and keeping your losses much, much smaller.

02:21.120 --> 02:23.700
So, how are we going to find those good entries?

02:23.700 --> 02:26.360
Well, it all starts with market structure.

02:26.740 --> 02:28.740
Market structure is very important.

02:29.360 --> 02:36.660
And if we're using any trading indicator, including Market Cipher, which is a great tool, we need to have something else.

02:36.740 --> 02:39.800
We need to have some kind of market structure to trade off of.

02:39.880 --> 02:42.460
CryptoFace, he uses spider lines.

02:42.560 --> 02:49.180
He uses other types of things, support and resistance levels to enter trades, to set targets and things of that nature.

02:49.180 --> 02:55.660
So, what I like to do is I like to mark out just horizontal levels of support and resistance starting on the daily time frame.

02:56.060 --> 02:59.360
And I know I go over this a lot, but it's important.

02:59.480 --> 03:00.300
It's very important.

03:00.480 --> 03:05.420
One of the most important things I think there is to finding good entries is having good levels of support and resistance.

03:05.420 --> 03:09.100
What you're going to do is you're going to pull up the daily time frame.

03:10.360 --> 03:13.260
You're going to start on the left of the chart.

03:14.000 --> 03:14.240
All right?

03:14.280 --> 03:15.040
Not over to the right.

03:15.100 --> 03:16.300
You're going to start in the past.

03:16.780 --> 03:20.960
And you're going to find areas where price has historically found a sticking point,

03:21.020 --> 03:23.200
where you see a lot of candle bodies opening and closing.

03:23.200 --> 03:27.500
One area I see is right here, around $46,500.

03:28.140 --> 03:37.160
We can see that this acted as a resistance for about three days before flipping into support for another four days.

03:37.220 --> 03:38.600
Then it was support again.

03:39.020 --> 03:40.120
It was support again.

03:40.260 --> 03:41.400
Then it got flipped into resistance.

03:41.640 --> 03:43.100
Then it got flipped into support again.

03:43.680 --> 03:45.880
And just recently, we came down and crashed.

03:45.940 --> 03:46.600
And where did we fall?

03:46.820 --> 03:47.320
What do you know?

03:47.660 --> 03:49.720
Right on this support area.

03:49.720 --> 03:56.560
Now, again, these lines, and I go into this more in the course, you should think of these more as zones, right?

03:56.600 --> 04:01.460
Not necessarily an exact price level, but more so a zone of liquidity, right?

04:01.520 --> 04:03.880
A zone of liquidity, you could draw a little box around it.

04:04.200 --> 04:11.340
And the reason it's a zone of liquidity is because these are areas where there's been a lot of liquid assets on the market.

04:11.600 --> 04:13.380
There's a lot of buying and selling happening.

04:13.820 --> 04:18.020
And price will tend to retrace back to those zones of high liquidity, right?

04:18.020 --> 04:29.160
When we come up here, whales want to push the price down here so that they can buy and fill those huge orders while the market is very liquid before we get another move to the upside, okay?

04:29.560 --> 04:36.940
So when we see these areas of support and resistance, we can expect that there is going to be some kind of a reaction off of them, right?

04:36.980 --> 04:37.540
A reaction.

04:37.700 --> 04:40.440
Look at what happens when price comes back down to this area.

04:41.000 --> 04:41.900
There's a reaction.

04:42.060 --> 04:43.340
It's acting as a support.

04:43.460 --> 04:45.300
It's flipping back and forth, support, resistance.

04:45.300 --> 04:50.340
When price comes to these areas, it gives us a lot of great opportunity to enter trades.

04:50.440 --> 04:51.360
They could be swing trades.

04:51.460 --> 04:52.320
They could be day trades.

04:52.480 --> 04:53.420
They could be scalp trades.

04:54.000 --> 04:57.400
I mean, just look at all the activity that happens here.

04:57.880 --> 04:59.880
This is the daily time frame, right?

05:00.120 --> 05:09.500
We could have come up and then shorted down and then we could have longed up, shorted down, longed up, shorted down in one day.

05:09.780 --> 05:12.300
Long up, short down, right?

05:12.300 --> 05:14.980
There's going to be a lot of opportunity to trade at these areas.

05:15.320 --> 05:20.720
And so you're going to mark out these daily levels where you see that price is found, a sticking point.

05:20.820 --> 05:25.300
So I can see another one, you know, very clearly around 49K, right?

05:26.300 --> 05:29.540
And then you could say we have another one right up here at about 52.

05:29.540 --> 05:34.560
And then I would say that we have another one right here around 54.

05:37.160 --> 05:51.860
And, of course, right now, you know, we could see that right here, these daily candles all the way back in February, right, are giving us the current resistance that we keep running up to in the past few days, you know, 55 something.

05:51.860 --> 06:01.380
Okay, and you're going to mark out these levels and you'll see that when you find them on the left, that they are respected in the future, right?

06:01.440 --> 06:02.440
They're respected in the future.

06:02.560 --> 06:04.480
Price will tend to come back to these areas.

06:05.280 --> 06:08.320
Okay, you don't have to have all these lines on your chart, right?

06:08.380 --> 06:14.400
You could maybe just have three because we're only trading between these zones right now, right?

06:14.400 --> 06:27.200
You don't have to have this all on your chart at the same time, and you can actually take these off or if you go to settings and go to visibility, you can choose what time frames you want to see these things on.

06:27.380 --> 06:30.660
So, for example, I might not want to see this on the one-day time frame.

06:31.700 --> 06:35.460
I might want to only see this on the two-day time frame or something of that nature.

06:36.160 --> 06:38.420
You can see that it goes away and comes back.

06:38.420 --> 06:46.760
Now, once we have these areas, okay, marked out on our chart, what we're going to do now is we're going to zoom into a lower time frame.

06:47.600 --> 06:53.900
And what we're going to look for is we are going to look for price to get to one of these areas, to get to one of these areas.

06:54.420 --> 06:59.540
And when price does get to one of these areas, what it does is it gives us a very good entry.

06:59.980 --> 07:00.680
Now, here's why.

07:00.680 --> 07:08.800
We know that if price is going to come up to this resistance, that it's probably going to get a rejection off the resistance and come back down.

07:09.540 --> 07:17.280
Now, whenever we enter a trade, it's very important that we are having a plan when we go in, right?

07:17.300 --> 07:20.720
We don't just want to enter a trade willy, willy, nilly, nilly, willy, nilly.

07:21.020 --> 07:23.100
No, we want to have a plan when we're coming in.

07:23.100 --> 07:27.580
So you might tell yourself earlier in the day, okay, I see Bitcoin's coming up.

07:27.960 --> 07:30.360
It's coming up above the $54K level.

07:30.540 --> 07:32.140
It's heading up to the $55K level.

07:32.400 --> 07:37.980
When we get to the $55K level, I am going to be looking for a short trade, okay?

07:38.840 --> 07:43.760
And so let's say we come up and we wick up right here to our resistance.

07:44.680 --> 07:47.560
And our plan is we're looking for a short, okay?

07:47.580 --> 07:48.320
This is our plan.

07:48.320 --> 07:54.760
Of course, we're going to use market cipher to tell us that the plan should be, you know, acted upon.

07:55.520 --> 08:03.680
But right now, what this does is once we start rejecting off of this resistance, our plan is we're trading the rejection.

08:03.860 --> 08:09.220
We're shorting the rejection, which means we have a very safe and effective area to place our stop loss.

08:09.340 --> 08:12.960
If I wait, if I zoom in maybe on the one-hour time frame, right?

08:12.960 --> 08:20.800
I zoom in to the one-hour time frame, and as soon as we're getting this rejection, let's say this purple candle, I see we're rejecting.

08:21.260 --> 08:22.360
I can enter right here.

08:22.760 --> 08:29.540
I can place my stop loss now above the last swing high, above the resistance, and I can set my take profit down at the support.

08:30.900 --> 08:33.640
What this does is it allows me to risk very little.

08:33.860 --> 08:35.820
I'm only risking 0.29%.

08:35.820 --> 08:42.500
If I have 100% of my account on this trade, I am risking only 0.29% of my entire account.

08:42.600 --> 08:46.100
This is a very, very good risk-to-reward ratio.

08:46.860 --> 08:49.320
And I'm potentially gaining almost 10%.

08:49.320 --> 08:50.520
I'm sorry.

08:50.640 --> 08:51.200
I'm sorry.

08:51.360 --> 08:52.480
Almost 3%.

08:52.480 --> 08:56.960
So I'm risking 0.2% to gain 3%.

08:56.960 --> 09:05.180
And the reason why this is a good entry, an objectively good entry, is because this resistance is the ceiling.

09:05.700 --> 09:17.640
If I'm assuming, and my plan is, that I'm shorting the rejection, that means if price comes above this last swing high, my plan becomes nullified and the trade becomes invalidated.

09:18.520 --> 09:21.780
Because my plan was wrong and I need to get out of the trade.

09:21.780 --> 09:22.840
It's very, very clear.

09:23.780 --> 09:32.760
It's very, very clear that I need to get out of the trade because this resistance is the line of demarcation between having me stay in this trade and having me get out of this trade.

09:33.300 --> 09:34.620
It becomes very, very clear.

09:35.380 --> 09:40.680
And so I can just stay in this trade, you know, until we come down to our support.

09:40.740 --> 09:42.660
And I might have take profits along the way, right?

09:42.680 --> 09:46.700
I might have take profit 1 here where I take out, you know, 50% of the trade.

09:46.700 --> 09:50.320
Then take profit 2 here where I take out another 20% of the trade.

09:50.820 --> 09:57.480
And then I let the other 30% ride out until we get to the final take profit and see how much I can get there.

09:58.300 --> 10:08.500
But my point is this is objectively a good trade because this stop loss is safe, meaning it's a good place to have a stop loss.

10:08.500 --> 10:17.600
Because I'm expecting us to have a reversal and it's safe, meaning it's going to give me enough wiggle room in case we have some volatility.

10:18.340 --> 10:21.840
But it's not going to be too big so that I risk too much.

10:21.900 --> 10:23.420
And it's an effective stop loss too.

10:23.860 --> 10:24.840
It's safe and effective.

10:24.840 --> 10:36.840
It's effective because it's allowing me to have enough wiggle room to come up above the last swing high while also giving me that wiggle room to have a little volatility and waiting for price to come back down.

10:36.840 --> 10:41.120
Now, what makes an entry not good, okay?

10:41.240 --> 10:47.520
Let's say that you wait until price comes all the way down here to short, okay?

10:48.040 --> 10:54.700
Because you are waiting for, let's say you're waiting for the one hour red trigger wave to confirm, okay?

10:56.500 --> 10:59.940
You can still enter here and you can still short down to the resistance.

11:00.180 --> 11:00.960
But here's the problem.

11:00.960 --> 11:10.440
In order for you to have a safe and effective stop loss, you would need to, after the confirmation of that red trigger wave, you would need to put your stop loss above the last swing high.

11:12.220 --> 11:16.520
And then set your take profit down here at the support.

11:17.120 --> 11:23.820
The reason you would need to do that is because there's a lot of volatility that can happen.

11:23.940 --> 11:25.480
Look at the volatility that happens here.

11:25.480 --> 11:29.520
We come down, we come back up, and we're coming back down again.

11:29.600 --> 11:30.760
We might even come back down lower.

11:31.280 --> 11:36.420
The point is this resistance is the line of demarcation that would invalidate the trade.

11:37.100 --> 11:43.040
We could come down here, get a retracement back up to here, and then come down here even more.

11:44.060 --> 11:45.040
And that could happen.

11:45.440 --> 11:49.240
Or, you know, we could come down here and then just come back up here.

11:49.240 --> 11:58.420
And so if that happens, if we get a reversal and your trade plan doesn't work out, you're risking more than you're potentially going to win on the trade.

11:59.360 --> 12:02.460
And a lot of people enter trades without actually thinking about this.

12:02.680 --> 12:05.720
They don't actually think about this because they don't have their levels marked.

12:06.240 --> 12:16.520
If we have our levels marked and we know what an objectively good entry is, then we can go down to market cipher and see, is it a good entry?

12:16.720 --> 12:17.760
And let's go find out.

12:17.820 --> 12:18.500
How do we do that?

12:18.500 --> 12:19.300
How do we do that?

12:19.700 --> 12:27.680
Well, you know I have a lot of strategies for trading with market cipher, algorithmic strategies, which are great, especially for beginners.

12:28.080 --> 12:31.440
But if you're more advanced, this is kind of what you want to do.

12:31.960 --> 12:39.940
When you see price coming up to one of these levels, okay, you want to go down to the lower time frames and see what the momentum is telling you.

12:40.300 --> 12:44.340
You want to go down to the three minute, okay, and see what the momentum is telling you.

12:44.400 --> 12:45.200
Let's check it out.

12:46.040 --> 12:46.940
Let's check it out.

12:48.500 --> 12:51.280
What is the momentum telling us?

12:52.220 --> 12:55.420
Well, we can see a few things here on the three minute time frame.

12:55.420 --> 12:59.220
Actually, let's go to the six minute time frame.

12:59.380 --> 13:01.320
You're going to have to look at multiple lower time frames.

13:01.400 --> 13:08.260
But on the three minute time frame, we can see something very clear, which is the fact that we're getting a downward direction.

13:08.260 --> 13:13.800
We're coming up to the resistance, and we can see market cipher is giving us a downward direction.

13:16.100 --> 13:29.300
We can clearly see that we were printing waves exclusively above the zero line, and now the waves are starting to come below the zero line, and we're forming lower red trigger waves right here, which is a sign that we are coming down.

13:29.300 --> 13:32.580
Okay, so you would know, okay, we're coming up to the resistance.

13:32.580 --> 13:34.460
My plan is to short the resistance.

13:34.460 --> 13:36.320
Is market cipher giving me confirmation?

13:37.020 --> 13:41.760
Yes, market cipher is giving me confirmation because I see the clear downward direction.

13:42.240 --> 13:43.100
And look at how it plays out.

13:43.160 --> 13:43.580
We come up.

13:43.720 --> 13:44.460
We reject once.

13:44.580 --> 13:46.020
We come up for the retest.

13:46.300 --> 13:48.440
Market cipher is showing us we're getting downward momentum.

13:48.440 --> 13:52.680
And then we come down below the resistance, and that's where we would enter our short trade.

13:52.840 --> 13:57.400
There's a number of options for the entry, but we can also go to the six minute time frame.

13:58.540 --> 14:06.440
And on the six minute time frame, you know, we can see also that momentum is coming down over time.

14:07.400 --> 14:12.500
Ideally, we would want to see a bearish divergence.

14:13.300 --> 14:15.340
I don't know if I necessarily see one here.

14:15.340 --> 14:21.940
I guess you could say that from this point right here to this point right here, the price is going up.

14:22.520 --> 14:26.900
And then from this point here to this point here, momentum is coming down a little bit.

14:27.300 --> 14:30.560
You could say that is a bearish divergence.

14:32.200 --> 14:37.720
Oftentimes, I would like to see a more clear divergence, but you don't even need to see the divergence all the time

14:37.720 --> 14:42.040
as long as you see market cipher is showing you the direction to trade in.

14:42.040 --> 14:45.520
And this can be done at supports and at resistances.

14:45.640 --> 14:47.260
So we come down to the resistance, right?

14:47.640 --> 14:51.240
We come down to the resistance, and what do we see on the six minute time frame?

14:51.580 --> 14:55.220
Well, we see kind of something very similar to what we saw at the resistance.

14:55.400 --> 14:57.340
We see that we get one touch.

14:58.340 --> 14:59.000
We come up.

14:59.060 --> 14:59.820
We get the retest.

14:59.920 --> 15:05.380
When we're getting the retest, we clearly see we're printing an anchor trigger,

15:05.380 --> 15:10.940
and we're seeing the money flow is starting to cross into the green from the red.

15:11.420 --> 15:13.940
Okay, so when we get that retest, that would be our entry.

15:15.000 --> 15:18.740
And look at this awesome, you know, entry that we have right here.

15:18.820 --> 15:21.820
We can set our stop loss right here below the last swing low.

15:22.140 --> 15:23.920
We're risking very little, 0.3%.

15:23.920 --> 15:27.280
And then we set our take profit up at the resistance.

15:28.280 --> 15:30.360
Now, again, we're not always going to hit our targets.

15:30.760 --> 15:31.420
We're not, okay?

15:31.500 --> 15:33.880
And that's why I'm a big fan of taking some profits out.

15:34.200 --> 15:37.180
I would take some out at resistances and supports that I see.

15:37.260 --> 15:38.260
So I would take one out here.

15:38.780 --> 15:41.360
Just going back in time to see where price finds a sticking point,

15:41.420 --> 15:42.860
I would probably take one out here.

15:43.980 --> 15:46.100
And then I would probably only hit two take profits,

15:46.380 --> 15:48.120
and I would still be in this trade right now.

15:48.200 --> 15:49.280
I would still be in this trade.

15:50.000 --> 15:51.100
There's a lot of options.

15:51.200 --> 15:53.700
You can either move your stop loss into break even,

15:53.700 --> 15:58.200
or you can move your stop loss into profit right here

15:58.200 --> 15:59.740
and let the thing play out.

16:00.000 --> 16:02.220
But now you're guaranteed to win a trade.

16:02.340 --> 16:03.420
You're locking in your profits.

16:03.500 --> 16:04.440
You're guaranteed to win a trade.

16:04.500 --> 16:06.420
And you started out with a really good entry, right?

16:06.980 --> 16:08.760
You're only risking 0.3%.

16:08.760 --> 16:10.560
If you're using 100% of your account,

16:10.700 --> 16:14.820
you're only risking 0.3% to potentially earn 3%.

16:14.820 --> 16:18.000
Okay, now I want to talk a little bit more

16:18.000 --> 16:21.600
about how you can manage the trade while it's open

16:21.600 --> 16:26.060
because locking in profit is part of risk management in my mind.

16:26.120 --> 16:26.600
Here's the thing.

16:26.900 --> 16:29.060
Nobody ever went broke taking profits, right?

16:29.560 --> 16:31.340
Nobody ever went broke taking profits.

16:32.020 --> 16:35.260
And so if you enter a trade, let's say you short right here.

16:35.920 --> 16:39.620
You short right here because you see the clear downward direction on Market Cipher.

16:40.140 --> 16:42.380
You enter your short right here.

16:42.960 --> 16:45.220
You set your stop loss above the last swing high.

16:46.360 --> 16:48.300
You set your final take profit down here.

16:48.300 --> 16:52.200
What you're going to do is you're going to go back in time

16:52.200 --> 16:55.100
and look for places where price has found a sticking point, right?

16:55.260 --> 16:58.140
Shorter-term support and resistance is on the six-minute time frame.

16:58.880 --> 17:00.700
I see clearly right here at this orange line,

17:00.800 --> 17:02.820
this was an area where price found a sticking point.

17:03.080 --> 17:04.600
So I'm going to set a take profit there.

17:05.120 --> 17:07.800
And then right here, I see price found a sticking point here.

17:07.820 --> 17:09.140
I'm going to set a take profit there.

17:09.720 --> 17:11.760
And then I'll leave this be my final take profit.

17:11.760 --> 17:15.520
What you are going to do to ensure that you are winning the trade

17:15.520 --> 17:18.540
is when you come down to your first take profit,

17:18.740 --> 17:21.260
you are going to take out some profits in Bybit, okay?

17:21.540 --> 17:24.020
And that's a pretty significant move right here, right?

17:24.060 --> 17:25.240
That's a 1% move.

17:25.320 --> 17:27.840
If you're using 3x leverage, that's a 3% move.

17:28.260 --> 17:33.300
You could take maybe anywhere from 25% to 50% of your trade out

17:33.300 --> 17:35.960
and let the rest ride.

17:36.080 --> 17:37.440
Once you do that, okay?

17:37.500 --> 17:40.020
Once you take that out, you have a few options.

17:40.020 --> 17:42.420
You can leave your stop loss where it is.

17:42.740 --> 17:45.220
And if you leave your stop loss where it is,

17:45.320 --> 17:50.220
you will still be able to exit that trade with a small win, right?

17:50.280 --> 17:56.260
Because you just made 1% and then lost 0.3%.

17:56.260 --> 18:02.100
So overall, you're up on that trade 0.7%, okay?

18:02.120 --> 18:03.060
Which is still a win.

18:03.500 --> 18:05.940
Or the other option you have once your first take profit gets hit

18:05.940 --> 18:07.700
is you move your stop loss into break even

18:07.700 --> 18:13.160
so that at the very least, you're winning 1% for the trade, okay?

18:13.520 --> 18:14.800
Those are the two options.

18:15.260 --> 18:19.340
What this does is even if you leave your stop loss where it is,

18:19.700 --> 18:21.420
you're still guaranteeing a winning trade

18:21.420 --> 18:23.720
simply because you took out that first take profit.

18:23.860 --> 18:26.360
But if you move this into break even,

18:26.620 --> 18:30.880
